{-
   What a `lens fn` declaration refuses, before any body is walked.

   Separate from `Reject.Scoped.yana` because of the resolver's phase order: every declaration is
   resolved before any body is, and a file whose declarations failed has no bodies walked - so one
   file holding both groups could only ever report the first.
-}

-- A lens body is a block with a `yield` in it. The `= expr` form is the explicit-callback
-- declaration, whose continuation's result is a free type variable in the signature.
lens fn expression() -> Int = 1

-- An iterator's body is a block for the same reason, and the message says which of the two was
-- written - `Iterator.yana` is the form that works.
iter fn counting(n: Int) -> Int = n

-- A teardown is called by the *compiler* when an object dies, so there is no call site to write a
-- continuation at - and `Drop` says so by declaring `fn drop`, which is what the message names. A
-- class may declare a `lens fn` member and this one does not, so the mistake is an implementation
-- of a signature nobody stated rather than a construct the compiler cannot represent.
data Held {id: Int}

instance Drop(Held):
    lens fn drop(->value: Held):
        yield {}

fn main() -> Int = 0
